# 10. AI ENTITIES, WORMHOLES AND FUTURE MULTI-PROVIDER ARCHITECTURE

This section is architectural preparation, not a request to fake integrations with external AI providers.

The long-term vision is that Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini and future providers can use the same Brain concurrently.

The Brain owns the knowledge.

External AI providers are independent entities that request authorized context and may submit new information.

## A. AI entity model

Prepare a provider-neutral entity abstraction.

Each entity should eventually have:

- Provider identity.
- Connection status.
- Permissions.
- Last communication.
- Access scope.
- Transfer history.
- Read/write capabilities.
- Error status.

Do not bind the core memory model to a single provider.

## B. Wormhole visualization

Prepare the graph architecture for external AI entities represented as distinct connected entities.

The eventual visualization should distinguish:

- Brain → AI: authorized context being sent.
- AI → Brain: information being received.
- Bidirectional communication.
- Inactive connections.
- Failed transfers.

The entity inspector should eventually show:

- Memories accessed.
- Memories sent.
- Information received.
- Nodes affected.
- Transfer timestamps.
- Permissions.
- Errors.

Do not fabricate live data flows.

Only visualize actual recorded transfers when the integration exists.

## C. Concurrent provider access

Design for multiple providers accessing the same Brain simultaneously.

Future requirements:

- Concurrent reads.
- Safe concurrent writes.
- Versioning.
- Conflict resolution.
- Idempotent operations.
- Duplicate protection.
- Per-provider permissions.
- Audit history.
- Isolation of private conversations.
- No provider ownership of shared memory.

One provider must never receive another provider's private information without explicit authorization.

Do not implement unsupported provider integrations in this phase.

